# 2. Make the relation between two classes explicit.
q0=qa(2,"recognise",1,[item("Which option directly relates the named classes in standard categorical form?",f"All {s} are {p}.",[(f"If a meeting starts, {s} arrive.","This is a conditional construction, not the requested standard class form."),(f"Are all {s} {p}?","This asks a question instead of making the claim."),(f"Please contact the {s}.","This is a request, not a categorical assertion.")],"The selected statement explicitly quantifies a subject class and attributes a predicate class.") for s,p in CONTEXTS])
q1=qa(2,"some",1,[item("In “Some "+s+" are "+p+",” what does Some establish?","At least one subject-class member belongs to the predicate class.",[("Every subject member belongs.","Some does not assert a universal claim."),("Exactly half the subject members belong.","Some fixes no such fraction."),("Some but certainly not all belong.","Some allows all to belong; it does not itself say not all.")],"In this logical use, some means at least one.") for s,p in CONTEXTS])
q2=qa(2,"no",2,[item("What does “No "+s+" are "+p+"” rule out?","Any member belonging to both named classes.",[("Every object outside both classes.","The claim concerns overlap, not everything outside both classes."),("Only one selected subject member.","No excludes overlap for the whole subject class."),("The existence of the predicate class everywhere.","The predicate class may exist outside the subject class.")],"No S are P denies overlap, rather than denying that P has any members anywhere.") for s,p in CONTEXTS])
q3=qa(2,"direction",2,[item("Suppose All "+s+" are "+p+". Which additional situation can still fit that claim?","Some "+p+" are outside the class of "+s+".",[("A subject-class member is outside the predicate class.","That is a direct counterexample to All S are P."),("No subject member can belong to the predicate class, even if a subject exists.","An existing subject must belong to P under the stated universal."),("The claim automatically reverses to All "+p+" are "+s+".","Inclusion need not be an equality or a reversible relation.")],"All S are P puts S inside P without exhausting P.") for s,p in CONTEXTS])
q4=repair(2,["Some means at least one in these exercises.","A categorical proposition relates subject and predicate classes.","All S are P does not automatically mean All P are S.","No S are P excludes overlap between the classes."],[("Some S are P necessarily means not all S are P.","Some is compatible with all; not all is an extra assertion."),("All S are P is automatically an argument with premises and a conclusion.","It is one proposition; an argument requires supporting claims and an inference."),("No S are P says that P cannot exist anywhere.","It excludes shared members, but P can have members outside S."),("An invented class example is automatically an actual rule at every school.","The model illustrates logical structure, not a verified nationwide fact.")])
lesson(2,"Categorical propositions","recognise a class-to-class assertion and explain what all, no and some commit it to",
 "identifying the subject, predicate, copula and quantifier","quantity, quality and the standard categorical forms",
 [("Relating classes","A categorical proposition affirms or denies membership relations between classes. All S are P says every S belongs to P. No S are P excludes shared members. Some S are P asserts at least one shared member; Some S are not P asserts at least one S outside P. S and P are class labels, not individual people’s initials in this use."),
  ("Read only what is asserted","For an invented Douala reading club, All members are badge holders allows badge holders who are not club members. It does not assert the reverse. Some members are readers means at least one, not exactly half and not necessarily fewer than all. No members are visitors excludes overlap; it does not prove that visitors do not exist."),
  ("Form and truth are separate","Translate the relation clearly before checking whether it is true. A false claim can still have categorical form. Under the modern diagram convention used later, universal claims alone do not assert that S exists; particular claims do. Traditional courses may add an existence assumption. State that assumption when needed rather than inferring a member from all alone.")],
 [("class","a collection picked out by a term"),("categorical proposition","a quantified assertion relating subject and predicate classes"),("some","at least one"),("overlap","members belonging to both classes")],("Some means some but never all.","Some means at least one and is compatible with all.","Not all is a further restriction absent from some alone."),
 worked({},"Does All club members are badge holders also say All badge holders are club members?",["The claim starts with the club-member class.","Each of its members must be a badge holder.","Other badge holders may exist outside that club."],"No. The original inclusion does not automatically reverse."),
 [card("l4-2-c1","A standard categorical assertion quantifies and relates two classes.",["All school gardens are cultivated areas has explicit class terms and a copula."],q0["id"]),card("l4-2-c2","Some means at least one, with no fixed upper limit.",["Some library books are loanable items does not itself say that others are not."],q1["id"]),card("l4-2-c3","No S are P rules out overlap.",["The predicate class can still have members outside S."],{**q2,"id":"l4-2-no-card","level":1})],
 [q0,q1,q2,q3,q4],"Categorical claims relate classes. All includes every S in P; no excludes overlap; some asserts existence. Some does not mean not all. An inclusion need not reverse, and a claim’s form does not establish its truth.",[1])

# 3. Separate quantity from quality; later combine them into the form labels.
q0=qa(3,"quantity",1,[item("Give the quantity of: "+x["text"],x["quantity"],[("particular" if x["quantity"]=="universal" else "universal","Check whether the claim covers all S or asserts at least one S."),("affirmative","Affirmative is a quality, not a quantity."),("negative","Negative is a quality, not a quantity.")],"The quantifier makes this a "+x["quantity"]+" proposition.") for x in R])
q1=qa(3,"quality",1,[item("Give the quality of: "+x["text"],x["quality"],[("negative" if x["quality"]=="affirmative" else "affirmative","Check whether membership is affirmed or denied."),("universal","Universal is a quantity, not a quality."),("particular","Particular is a quantity, not a quality.")],"The relation is "+x["quality"]+"; the quantifier alone does not settle quality.") for x in R])
q2=qa(3,"combine",2,[item("Give the quantity and quality of: "+x["text"],LABEL[x["code"]],[(label,"That combination does not match both the quantifier and the affirmation/denial in this sentence.") for c,label in LABEL.items() if c!=x["code"]],"The quantifier makes it "+x["quantity"]+" and the membership relation "+x["quality"]+".") for x in R])
q3=qa(3,"change",2,[item("Bih changes “All "+s+" are "+p+"” to “Some "+s+" are "+p+".” What changes?","Quantity changes; quality stays affirmative.",[("Quality changes; quantity stays universal.","All becomes some, changing scope rather than affirmation."),("Both become negative.","Neither sentence denies membership."),("Neither quantity nor quality changes.","The universal-to-particular change is significant.")],"All and some differ in quantity, while both sentences affirm membership.") for s,p in CONTEXTS])
q4=repair(3,["Quantity distinguishes universal and particular scope.","Quality distinguishes affirmation and denial.","No S are P is universal and negative.","Some S are P is particular and affirmative."],[("No S are P is particular because it contains no objects.","No covers the whole subject class by denying any overlap; it is universal in quantity."),("A negative proposition must be an insulting remark.","Negative is a logical denial of membership, not a description of politeness."),("Every particular claim is negative.","Some S are P is particular and affirmative."),("All and some differ only in spelling, without a change in scope.","All concerns the whole subject class; some asserts at least one member.")])
lesson(3,"Properties: quantity and quality","identify quantity and quality independently and explain which property changes in a rewrite",
 "categorical claims and their quantifiers","combining the properties to recognise A, E, I and O",
 [("Quantity asks how widely","Universal propositions concern the entire subject class: All S are P and No S are P. Particular propositions assert at least one subject member: Some S are P and Some S are not P. Universal does not mean that every object in the world is included. It concerns the whole class named by the subject."),
  ("Quality asks whether membership is affirmed","Affirmative propositions attribute membership: All S are P and Some S are P. Negative propositions deny it: No S are P and Some S are not P. Negative does not mean rude, undesirable or false. A negative statement can be polite and true. Notice that No is universal in quantity despite denying overlap."),
  ("Track a change precisely","In an invented Bamenda club example, changing All members are readers to Some members are readers changes quantity while keeping affirmative quality. Changing Some members are readers to Some members are not readers changes quality while keeping particular quantity. Classify each property separately before combining them; the next lesson names the resulting patterns.")],
 [("quantity","universal or particular scope"),("quality","affirmation or denial of membership"),("universal","covering the whole subject class"),("particular","asserting at least one subject member")],("No makes a claim particular.","No S are P is universal and negative.","It rules out overlap for the entire subject class."),
 worked({},"Analyse Some library books are not loanable items.",["Some asserts at least one subject member: particular quantity.","Are not denies membership: negative quality.","Keep these two properties separate before naming the whole form."],"Particular quantity and negative quality."),
 [card("l4-3-c1","All and no are universal; some is particular.",["No school gardens are cultivated areas is universal in quantity, even if factually false."],q0["id"]),card("l4-3-c2","Affirmation and denial determine quality.",["Some S are P affirms; Some S are not P denies membership."],q1["id"]),card("l4-3-c3","Changing all to some changes scope, not affirmation.",["All S are P and Some S are P remain affirmative."],{**q3,"id":"l4-3-change-card","level":1})],
 [q0,q1,q2,q3,q4],"Quantity asks universal or particular; quality asks affirmative or negative. No S are P is universal negative. Analyse both properties independently, then check what a rewrite preserves or changes.",[2])

# 4. The four forms; computed counterexamples prevent pure label memorisation.
